content.thinking:
I accidentally introduced a reference to a nonexistent `BidiIsolateValidatorHelper.fiveAfterTwoLetters()` and still have literal invisible chars. My output pipeline keeps converting `\uXXXX` sequences into actual characters. I need a reliable way to write escape sequences. 

Option: build the strings in Java without literals, e.g. `new String(Character.toChars(0x2067))` or constants:

```java
private static final String LRI = "\u2066"; // would still be literal...
```

Use `Character.toString(0x2067)` — no escapes needed at all:

```java
private static final String LRI = Character.toString(0x2066);
private static final String RLI = Character.toString(0x2067);
private static final String FSI = Character.toString(0x2068);
private static final String PDI = Character.toString(0x2069);
```

Then compose: `"A" + RLI + "B" + PDI`. This is fully robust and readable. Rewrite the whole test that way.

